Relevant columns and their possible usage  

• "SALES_ORDERS"."OrderID": primary key for an order – links to "SALES_ORDERLINES" and to "SALES_INVOICES"."OrderID".  
• "SALES_ORDERS"."CustomerID": identifies the customer who placed each order – base column for customer–level grouping.  
• "SALES_ORDERLINES"."OrderID": foreign‑key to the parent order – lets us aggregate order‑line information back to the order and customer.  
• "SALES_ORDERLINES"."StockItemID", "Quantity", "UnitPrice": build the extended value of each order line.  
• "SALES_INVOICES"."InvoiceID": primary key for an invoice – links to "SALES_INVOICELINES".  
• "SALES_INVOICES"."OrderID": tells which order the invoice was raised against – the bridge from order to invoice.  
• "SALES_INVOICES"."CustomerID": customer on the invoice – should match the order’s customer.  
• "SALES_INVOICELINES"."InvoiceID": foreign‑key – allows aggregation of invoice lines to an invoice (and to a customer through "SALES_INVOICES").  
• "SALES_INVOICELINES"."StockItemID", "Quantity", "UnitPrice": build the extended value of each invoice line.  

Typical conditions that may be required  

• Join "SALES_ORDERS" → "SALES_INVOICES" on "OrderID" (and check the same "CustomerID").  
• Join "SALES_ORDERLINES" → "SALES_INVOICELINES" through ("SALES_ORDERLINES"."OrderID" = "SALES_INVOICES"."OrderID" AND "SALES_INVOICES"."InvoiceID" = "SALES_INVOICELINES"."InvoiceID").  
• Aggregate order‑lines and invoice‑lines by customer and compare counts / sums.  
• Use HAVING clauses to keep only customers where order and invoice counts ‑ and extended totals – are identical.  

Below are ten explorative Snowflake SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows) that gradually move from simple inspection to a final query returning the customer count that fulfils the stated condition.

```sql
-- 1) Description: Peek at orders – customer and order identifiers
SELECT 
    "OrderID",
    "CustomerID"
FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_ORDERS"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Description: Peek at invoices – customer and their source order
SELECT 
    "InvoiceID",
    "OrderID",
    "CustomerID"
FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_INVOICES"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Description: Show how many invoices exist for each order (sanity check)
SELECT 
    o."OrderID",
    COUNT(i."InvoiceID") AS "InvoiceCnt"
FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_ORDERS"  o
LEFT JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_INVOICES" i
       ON i."OrderID" = o."OrderID"
GROUP BY o."OrderID"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Description: Inspect order‑lines with a simple extended value
SELECT 
    l."OrderLineID",
    l."OrderID",
    l."StockItemID",
    l."Quantity",
    l."UnitPrice",
    (l."Quantity" * l."UnitPrice") AS "OrderLineValue"
FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_ORDERLINES" l
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Description: Inspect invoice‑lines with an extended value
SELECT 
    il."InvoiceLineID",
    il."InvoiceID",
    il."StockItemID",
    il."Quantity",
    il."UnitPrice",
    (il."Quantity" * il."UnitPrice") AS "InvoiceLineValue"
FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_INVOICELINES" il
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Description: Link an order‑line to its matching invoice‑line (same stock item) through the order & invoice tables
SELECT 
    o."CustomerID",
    l."OrderID",
    l."StockItemID",
    l."Quantity"  AS "OrderQty",
    il."Quantity" AS "InvoiceQty",
    (l."Quantity" * l."UnitPrice")  AS "OrderValue",
    (il."Quantity" * il."UnitPrice") AS "InvoiceValue"
FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_ORDERLINES"   l
JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_ORDERS"       o  ON o."OrderID"       = l."OrderID"
JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_INVOICES"     i  ON i."OrderID"       = o."OrderID"
JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_INVOICELINES" il ON il."InvoiceID"     = i."InvoiceID"
                                                                       AND il."StockItemID" = l."StockItemID"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Description: Aggregate order‑line values per customer
SELECT 
    o."CustomerID",
    COUNT(DISTINCT l."OrderID")                AS "OrderCnt",
    SUM(l."Quantity" * l."UnitPrice")          AS "OrderTotal"
FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_ORDERLINES" l
JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_ORDERS"     o
     ON o."OrderID" = l."OrderID"
GROUP BY o."CustomerID"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Description: Aggregate invoice‑line values per customer
SELECT 
    i."CustomerID",
    COUNT(DISTINCT i."InvoiceID")              AS "InvoiceCnt",
    SUM(il."Quantity" * il."UnitPrice")        AS "InvoiceTotal"
FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_INVOICES"     i
JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_INVOICELINES" il
     ON il."InvoiceID" = i."InvoiceID"
GROUP BY i."CustomerID"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Description: Combine the two aggregates and retain customers whose
--                 order‑side counts & totals exactly equal the invoice side
SELECT
    co."CustomerID",
    co."OrderCnt",
    ci."InvoiceCnt",
    co."OrderTotal",
    ci."InvoiceTotal"
FROM (
        SELECT 
            o."CustomerID",
            COUNT(DISTINCT l."OrderID")       AS "OrderCnt",
            SUM(l."Quantity" * l."UnitPrice") AS "OrderTotal"
        FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_ORDERLINES" l
        JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_ORDERS"     o
             ON o."OrderID" = l."OrderID"
        GROUP BY o."CustomerID"
     ) co
JOIN (
        SELECT
            i."CustomerID",
            COUNT(DISTINCT i."InvoiceID")      AS "InvoiceCnt",
            SUM(il."Quantity" * il."UnitPrice") AS "InvoiceTotal"
        FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_INVOICES"     i
        JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_INVOICELINES" il
             ON il."InvoiceID" = i."InvoiceID"
        GROUP BY i."CustomerID"
     ) ci
  ON ci."CustomerID" = co."CustomerID"
WHERE co."OrderCnt"    = ci."InvoiceCnt"
  AND co."OrderTotal"  = ci."InvoiceTotal"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Description: Count how many customers meet the equality condition
SELECT 
    COUNT(*) AS "Customers_Matching_Order_Invoice_Count_And_Value"
FROM (
    SELECT
        co."CustomerID"
    FROM (
            SELECT 
                o."CustomerID",
                COUNT(DISTINCT l."OrderID")       AS "OrderCnt",
                SUM(l."Quantity" * l."UnitPrice") AS "OrderTotal"
            FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_ORDERLINES" l
            JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_ORDERS"     o
                 ON o."OrderID" = l."OrderID"
            GROUP BY o."CustomerID"
         ) co
    JOIN (
            SELECT
                i."CustomerID",
                COUNT(DISTINCT i."InvoiceID")       AS "InvoiceCnt",
                SUM(il."Quantity" * il."UnitPrice") AS "InvoiceTotal"
            FROM WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_INVOICES"     i
            JOIN WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS.WIDE_WORLD_IMPORTERS."SALES_INVOICELINES" il
                 ON il."InvoiceID" = i."InvoiceID"
            GROUP BY i."CustomerID"
         ) ci
      ON ci."CustomerID" = co."CustomerID"
    WHERE co."OrderCnt"   = ci."InvoiceCnt"
      AND co."OrderTotal" = ci."InvoiceTotal"
) q;
```

These ten queries progress from basic data discovery to an aggregated answer. Each can be executed independently to understand intermediate results, check assumptions, and finally obtain the required count of customers satisfying the stated matching criteria.
